  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Friday)\nIssued at 1146 PM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nSubtropical ridging anchored over the region will maintain dry\nand unseasonably warm conditions through the weekend. Afternoon\nhighs are expected to reach the low to mid 70s, running 10 to\n15 degrees above normal. As surface high pressure shifts east on\nSaturday, return flow will re-establish, pushing dewpoints into\nthe 40s and lower 50s as low-level moisture increases.\n\nA longwave trough traversing the Northern Plains early Sunday\nwill displace the lingering ridge. Concurrently, a shortwave will\ntrack east from the Southern Plains into the Lower Mississippi\nValley by Sunday afternoon. The combination of returning moisture\nand a fast-moving cold front will generate scattered showers. QPF\nremains light, with NBM guidance indicating total rainfall\nbetween 0.10 and 0.35 inches across most of the area.\n\nCool, dry continental air will build into the Mid-South behind\nthe front through midweek. Temperatures will drop slightly below\nseasonal averages, featuring highs in the low to mid 50s and\novernight lows in the 30s. Long-range ensemble guidance (LREF)\nsuggests a transition toward a more active CONUS pattern during\nThanksgiving week. However, limited moisture availability-with\nPWAT values hovering around 0.5 inches-will keep precipitation\npotential low.\n\nAC3",
